  "worked_solution": "First, we compute the partial derivatives of $f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine the statements one-by-one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function $f$ is defined at $(0,2)$, where we have \n\n$$\nf(0,2)=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({0}^{2}+{2}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}\u20612,\n$$\n\nand both partial derivatives are also defined at $(0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,2) & =\\frac{2(0)}{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}-2}=0 \\\\ {f}_{y}(0,2) & =\\frac{2(2)}{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}-2}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If we substitute $(1,0)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,0)=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({1}^{2}+{0}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(-1)\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since $\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x$ is defined only for $x>0.$ Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\n- Statement III is also false. If we substitute $(1,1)$ into $f$, we get\n\n$$\nf(1,0)=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({1}^{2}+{1}^{2}-2)=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(0)\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined since $\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x$ is defined only for $x>0.$ Since the function is not defined at the point, the derivative is also undefined.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\".",
